WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.660
Have you ever travelled to Spain?

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.010
Have you ever visited Barcelona, Madrid or
the Canary Islands?

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:16.270
Well, if you have, I’m sure there is something
that might have surprised you.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:20.080
Despite the fact that Spain is in a crisis
and has the second highest unemployment rate

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.930
in all of Europe, it totally looks like a
wealthy country.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.970
The cities are clean, the roads have brand
new pavement, there are international airports

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:31.449
in every other city and there’s a state
of the art high speed train throughout most

00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:34.220
of the country.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:39.730
At least on the surface, Madrid has no reasons
to be jealous of Zurich or London.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:45.180
Then again, we are talking about a country
that was at the edge of bankruptcy not such

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:51.110
a long time ago and that now has gone through
one of the biggest political crises in their

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:52.489
recent history.

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:57.610
The Spanish Premier, Mariano Rajoy will have
to leave the Government.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.289
The Congress has passed an impeachment bill.

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:04.280
Impeachment means that the Congress, that
is, the legislative power, votes to kick the

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.520
executive out.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.970
Unlike other countries like America, in Spain
you need to propose an alternative government

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.960
that will substitute for the former.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.000
This makes impeachment attempts almost impossible
to pull off in this country.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.680
But these are strange days in Spanish political
life.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.860
The brand new Spanish president, the social
democrat Pedro Sanchez has managed to convince

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:29.130
the radical left, the nationalist right, and
the independent Catalonian parties to vote

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.060
against the conservative Government of Mariano
Rajoy.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.470
They all have one thing in common: they didn’t
want more of this.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:41.850
And now you might wonder…

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.049
Why did Rajoy have so many haters?

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:45.010
Well… you might think it’s corruption.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.050
And, of course, this is the official reason.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:50.270
But beyond the surface is way more.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.190
As we said before, Spain is one of the most
damaged countries from the big financial crisis

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.329
of 2008.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:59.670
And the former government likes to brag about
their economic achievement with statements

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.189
like this one.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:08.930
“It has been a honor to leave this country
in a better shape than I found it” –Mariano

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:09.930
Rajoy

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.019
This is why, in today’s video, we have a
lot of questions cover.

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:15.690
First… has Spain gotten over the crisis
already?

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.980
Despite some economic growth and unemployment
reduction, this country still has more than

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.500
a 16% unemployment rate.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.430
And other EURO countries like Ireland are
growing faster than Spain.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.090
So… can we really say they have left the
financial crisis behind?

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.790
And if so… what credit should we give to
the conservative government?

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:36.750
Is it a good moment to invest in Spain?

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.950
Today, we are going to answer all of these
questions but before we do, let’s take a

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.530
look back at history.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.569
MEN IN BLACK

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:48.650
In the year 2002, the EUROZONE was created.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:53.620
All of a sudden, Spain, Germany, France and
many other countries started to use the same

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.450
currency, the EURO, and the same interest
rates, the ones that the European Central

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:58.790
Bank fixes.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.620
This seemed to be a great idea.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.100
Having the same currency, a German bank can
lend money to a Spanish company on the same

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.310
conditions as they would with a German company.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.620
But these advantages come with a risk.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.209
If Spain goes bankrupt, this German bank,
with Spanish debt, might go bankrupt too.

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:22.709
In other words, if a country does poorly,
they might spread the problems to other countries.

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:26.269
This is why when you join the EURO, you must
follow some rules.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:29.660
One of them is that no country can have a
deficit bigger than 3%.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.600
This means they cannot spend more than what
they collect in taxes.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.670
This said, let’s go fast forward in history.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.860
2008, year of the international financial
crisis.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.450
Many European countries suffer.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.490
Spain among them.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.500
Suddenly, the unemployment rate goes sky high
til it’s surpassed 20%.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.430
This means that, all of a sudden, the Government
collects little money in taxes and…

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.620
Do you remember those international airports
and high speed trains I told you about?

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.810
Well, they are not cheap.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.480
And this is why the inevitable happened.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:05.519
Spain, Greece and Ireland had the highest
budget deficits in the EU in 2012

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:11.440
So, while the maximum deficit a Euro country
could have was 3%, Spain had 10%.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.920
This meant nobody wanted to lend money to
their Government.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.920
At the end of the day…

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.799
How are you going to pay it back if you spend
10% more than what you collect?

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:23.210
If you’ve followed VisualPolitik for a long
time, you already know what happens when investors

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:26.480
don’t trust your credit reliability, right?

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:27.530
What do they do…?

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.530
Exactly!

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.700
They ask for higher interest rates.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:36.540
Now listen, in the EUROZONE, we have an index
to measure the chances of a country defaulting:

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.750
the so-called RISK PREMIUM.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:48.280
Basically, what they do is take the most trustworthy
debt bond that exists in Europe.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:49.750
This is the GERMAN bond.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:54.150
Then, they compare the interest rate of the
German Bond with the interest rate of the

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.150
other ones.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.560
The number that you get with this comparison
is called the RISK PREMIUM.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.699
So in the year 2010, the Spanish debt had
a risk premium of 50 points.

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:05.120
That was OK.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.919
One year later, it had 250.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:11.580
5 times more.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.750
Spanish Bonds at Risk as Contagion Gathers
Force

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.570
And this is when the MEN IN BLACK came into
picture.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:22.200
Yes, the men in back was the name given by
the journalist to the European Central Bank

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.200
workers.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.790
These ECB people went to the most problematic
countries in the EUrozone and requested that

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:29.860
they follow the rules.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.650
Basically: it says right here that you can’t
have a deficit bigger than 3% but you’re

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:34.759
having it your way.

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:38.330
Stop wasting money like crazy or we’ll punish
you.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:43.130
In countries like Ireland, these men in black
were especially strict.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:47.590
In some, like Italy, they even forced the
parliament to choose another prime minister

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:49.380
who was more fiscally responsible.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.790
But… what happened in Spain?

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:58.229
What did these men in black do in the country
of sangria and paella?

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:08.680
Let’s have a look!

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.380
THE QUIET MAN

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.830
November 20th, 2011.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:17.730
This is the day when the Spanish People’s
Party kicks ass in the elections.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:22.680
The conservative leader, Mariano Rajoy becomes
one of the most supported leaders in Europe,

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.289
only surpassed by Viktor Orban in Hungary.

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:30.259
The Spanish People’s Party or, as it’s
better known, the PP, got 53% of the seats

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:34.110
in Congress and they ruled in most of the
regional governments.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:41.340
Basically, Mr. Rajoy might as well have changed
the name of Spain and started calling it RAJOYLAND.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.340
He’d have had enough support for it.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.660
So what was his political program during the
elections?

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:48.460
Well… basically, Rajoy said the European
Central Bank ‘Hey guys!

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:51.460
Don’t worry, I’m conservative so I know
my economics!

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:56.120
First I’m gonna reduce taxes and make lots
of cool business friendly reforms that would

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.139
make this country look like Silicon fucking
Valley.

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:03.280
Then those companies will make billions, hire
people, and booom!

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.250
Deficit problem solved!’

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:14.620
Basically, the whole Rajoy campaign was based
on the idea of low taxes.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:21.699
The mantra for any problem was to reduce taxes
on everything.

00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:34.789
But, my dear viewers… you know that what
you say during the electoral campaign is one

00:07:34.789 --> 00:07:40.720
thing, and that what you do just ONE DAY after
you take office is another thing entirely.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:46.370
“This government must raise some taxes for
some time”

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:53.560
During the first months of Rajoy, the Government
raised ALL of the taxes, including VAT, income

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.080
tax and all the special taxes on tobacco and
gasoline.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.400
Yes, a conservative Government made the biggest
tax increase in Spanish history.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.690
And what about all those wonderful business
regulations that would turn Spain into the

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.150
new Silicon Valley?

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.880
Well… you can imagine what happened with
that too.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:13.660
So what happens when you promise a business
friendly agenda and come up with the opposite

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:14.830
once you’re in office?

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:18.810
Well… voters, angry as they are, cannot
do anything until the next elections.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:24.410
But the international investors… they can
give you their backs the very moment you break

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.569
their trust.

00:08:25.569 --> 00:08:29.240
And this is exactly what happened in Spain.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.360
Spanish economy in "huge crisis" after credit
downgrade

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.410
Basically, the Spanish debt bond fell under
the category of trash in just a glance.

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:39.810
Do you remember the RISK PREMIUM I told you
about before?

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:44.740
Well, so at some point, it went to the Satanic
figure of… 666.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:48.700
Many Spanish banks where ot the edge of bankruptcy.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.800
And nobody wanted to buy Spanish debt.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.550
Spain was about to collapse.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.820
And that would mean no money to pay doctors,
school teachers and tax collectors.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.920
Only one team could solve this problem.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:04.500
And they were… the MEN IN BLACK.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.100
Spain to Accept Rescue From Europe for Its
Ailing Banks

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.870
So what was this bailout all about?

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:14.600
Basically, Europe would borrow money at a
lower interest rate so Spanish banks would

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:15.600
not go bankrupt.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.380
In exchange, they requested the Government
to cut its spending.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:26.519
And here is where Mariano Rajoy did his big,
badass movement or as we like to call it,

00:09:26.519 --> 00:09:27.519
RAJOY-OMICS.

00:09:27.519 --> 00:09:30.550
Basically, once the European Central Bank
had already bailed out the banks, Rajoy went

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:32.320
to those men in black and said…

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.690
‘Hey, you, ECB guys… you think you can
tell me what to do?

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:38.330
Look, see these three fingers here???

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.250
Read between the lines.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:44.030
Rajoy’s theory was that Spain was too big
to fail.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:47.930
So, despite having the worst credit rate you
can imagine, the European Central Bank would

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:52.050
eventually buy all the debt bonds without
asking for any interest rate.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.050
How?

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.130
Printing money!

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:58.960
With this Money, Spain could keep being as
it was without making any costly reforms.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.010
But why would the ECB accept all of this?

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.010
Easy…

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.959
Spain has the 4th biggest economy in Europe.

00:10:05.959 --> 00:10:08.110
Nobody’s gonna let it fail.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:11.140
So months kept passing.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.839
Unemployment kept growing and so did the Risk
Premium.

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:19.660
The Conservative government of the People’s
Party, far from making business friendly reforms,

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:20.839
did the opposite.

00:10:20.839 --> 00:10:23.400
Do you want an example?

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:24.940
Here it is.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:30.350
Prior to them, professional services like
taxi drivers where going to be liberalized.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.790
But, in Spain, taxi driver associations are
well connected with the conservatives.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:39.470
So the right wing passed a bill to protect
taxi drivers from competition.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:45.380
This is why, many sharing economy companies
like UBER and AirBNB now have so many restrictions

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:46.380
in the Spanish market.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:50.230
The question is… do you think Rajoy’s
challenge to the European Central Bank worked?

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.230
And the answer is…

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:53.230
YES.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:11:05.580
“The ECB is Ready to do whatever it takes
to preserve the euro and believe me, it will

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.500
be enough”

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.130
SUPER MARIO COMES TO THE RESCUE

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:15.890
For months, the ECB president, Mario Draghi,
said that he would not compromise with Spain.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:20.130
Basically, Rajoy wanted him to print more
money so he could buy Spanish debt.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:25.120
In other words, Rajoy wanted the wealthy EURO
countries like Germany and Finland to give

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.010
money to Spain at no political cost.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.070
Just because… reasons.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:32.130
It doesn’t sound very fair, does it?

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.779
Well… sometimes life is not fair.

00:11:34.779 --> 00:11:36.990
And Rajoy was right.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:40.430
Spain was too big to fail.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:45.519
ECB could be ready to buy weak Italian, Spanish
bonds

00:11:45.519 --> 00:11:49.470
Since then, 25% of the Spanish debt is in
ECB hands.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:53.820
And those words from Mario Draghi, saying
he would do ‘whatever it takes to save the

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:55.250
Euro’ were like magic.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:59.950
One day at a time, the Risk Prime of Spain
started falling.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:03.899
Investors started buying debt bonds because
they knew they had the backing of the ECB.

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:08.600
And this is how Spain could keep piling on
more and more debt.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:13.950
This country now has a debt that represents
more than 100% of their entire GDP.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:18.670
Meanwhile, the rest of the European countries
have started growing.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:26.670
Since 2012, Germany has become the biggest
export powerhouse in the entire world.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:31.350
China has gone from being the World’s factory
to developing a brand new consumer market

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:33.390
where everybody can sell their products.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:36.139
In other words, EVERY European country is
growing now.

00:12:36.139 --> 00:12:38.010
And that, of course, includes Spain.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.450
Nonetheless, this was good news.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:43.300
And every government likes good news.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:48.810
Rajoy: "Spain has gotten over the crisis in
record time"

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:49.990
This is already 2015.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.339
In this year Spain has elections again.

00:12:52.339 --> 00:12:57.329
And the government decides to celebrate the
end of the crisis with a tax cut in the middle

00:12:57.329 --> 00:12:58.709
of the electoral campaign.

00:12:58.709 --> 00:13:00.310
Brilliant, right?

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:04.100
You raise taxes at the beginning of one election
and then, one year before the next election,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.010
you put them where they used to be.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:09.300
And then, guess what happened right after
Rajoy won the elections again!

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:13.050
Yup, you got it right!

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:18.839
Rajoy raises taxes… again
In 2017, the Government will have to cut 15

00:13:18.839 --> 00:13:19.839
billions deficit.

00:13:19.839 --> 00:13:24.350
As you can imagine, raising and reducing taxes
depending on when you have elections is not

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:27.519
the best policy if you want to attract foreign
investors.

00:13:27.519 --> 00:13:31.970
Add red tape that’s almost the same, if
not more prevalent than before and all the

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:35.920
territorial problems and you’ll understand
why the unemployment rate in Spain is still

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:38.590
at 16%.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:46.680
While other EURO countries like ESTONIA and
IRELAND, which had really bad recessions,

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:52.390
are now way better than 10 years ago, Spain
remains almost the same.

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.680
So now it’s your turn…

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:58.500
Do you think the brand new Spanish Government
can do any worse than the previous one?

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:02.290
Do you think Spain has solved its crisis or
do you think there’s still a lot of work

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:03.290
to do there?

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:05.550
Please, leave your answer in the comments
section below.

